# Cheaper walk map refresh (O225; server/README.md "Cheaper walk map refresh (O225)").
# A walking monster keeps a 33 x 25 grid of "can I step here" answers (Creature::localMapCache, Monster::useCacheMap).
# Every step Creature::onCreatureMove shifts the grid and fills the new edge, a column of 25 or a row of 33 tiles, with
# one Map::getTile each: a walk down the quad tree from the root (12 pointer hops, a likely cache miss each) although
# neighbouring tiles of an 8x8 block share one leaf and one Floor. Creature::updateMapCache (first think, teleport, floor
# change) does that for all 825 tiles.
# Now:
#   * src/chaosstrip.h (new, header only, no engine types; server/tools/chaosstrip_test.cpp and
#     server/tools/strip_bench.cpp build it alone): ChaosStrip::TileReader, the same tile as Map::getTile (arguments
#     narrowed to 16, 16 and 8 bits the same way) with the Floor of the current 8x8 block kept in two words on the stack
#     of one refresh. Nothing is kept between calls (a tile or floor added later is always seen), nothing is allocated.
#   * creature.cpp: the five Map::getTile calls of the edge refresh and of updateMapCache read through a TileReader over
#     Map::getFloorAt (patch 0072). updateTileCache and its queryAdd are untouched.
# The tiles are the same ones in the same order: the test compares Map::getTile with the reader on many random maps,
# columns, rows and full grids, other floors, missing floors and leaves, and the edges of the 16 bit coordinates.
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/chaosstrip.h
@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
